         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Images from Teotihuacan\n\nThese images are copyright for Teotihuacan and allowed to be used according to the license determined by them.\n\nTo visualize all images, use the left and right arrows on the slide below.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div class=\"htSlides\"><div class=\"htSlide\"><div class=\"htSlideCounter\">1 / 3</div><img class=\"imgGameSize\" id=\"metate3\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('metate3', '0%')\"><div class=\"htSlideCaption\">Metate from Mexico. Photo taken on <htdate0>, during a visit to the Archaeological Site of Teotihuacan, México (<htcite0>).</div><div class=\"htSlideRefs\"><b>Related content:</b><ul><li>Playing with Consonants and Vowels - An interactive game to understand how syllables are created. (<htcite1>)</li><li>Multiplying the Findings - an educational game in which the reader visualizes multiplication while exploring different archaeological sites. (<htcite2>)</li><li>Sequence game (Part I) - In this first stage, learning is reinforced by completing number sequences that go up to the hundreds, helping to consolidate the understanding of basic foundations. (<htcite3>)</li><li>Sequence game (Part II) - Continuing the challenge, this stage presents sequences that extend up to the billions, deepening the mastery of numerical patterns. (<htcite4>)</li><li>Limits and contexts - a common point between science and writing is the fact that people do not always understand their limits. But how can we identify them? (<htcite5>)</li><li>Are we truly equal? - In this text, we explore the acceptable limits of inequality and emphasize that within our species, there are no races. (<htcite6>)</li><li>Always present - many times we are so focused on ourselves or on opposites that we do not notice what is always present. (<htcite7>)</li><li>Cooking - The preparation of food is often remembered for adding quantities, but there is also another side to it. (<htcite8>)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"htSlide\"><div class=\"htSlideCounter\">2 / 3</div><img class=\"imgGameSize\" id=\"ChronologyTeotihuacan\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('ChronologyTeotihuacan', '0%')\"><div class=\"htSlideCaption\">General view of Teotihuacan. Photograph taken on <htdate1>, during a visit to the site (<htcite9>).</div><div class=\"htSlideRefs\"><b>Related content:</b><ul><li>Walking Among the letters - In this text, we explore the relationships between integers and texts. (<htcite10>)</li><li>Walking Among the Numbers - In this text, we explore the relationships between integers. (<htcite11>)</li><li>I live on the planet... - It is not only time that has different counts. When we speak of the planet we live on, we also have different ideas about it. (<htcite12>)</li><li>Understanding the Multiplication Table (0 and 1) - Many times, the multiplication table is presented as something to be memorized — but, why should we ignore the knowledge that comes with it? (<htcite13>)</li><li>Understanding the Multiplication Tables (Summary) - After studying 10 multiplication tables, we will review the content. But now, we will follow the opposite direction, starting with charts until we reach the general formula. (<htcite14>)</li><li>Chronology - Is it possible to create a universal timeline applicable to all of humanity? (<htcite15>)</li><li>The result is (Phase II) - a game to reinforce the connection between addition and multiplication. (<htcite16>)</li><li>Translation game (Part I) - Explore numbers from different cultures through an engaging translation game. (<htcite17>)</li><li>The result is - a game of consecutive sums that helps uncover the story. (<htcite18>)</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"htSlide\"><div class=\"htSlideCounter\">3 / 3</div><img class=\"imgGameSize\" id=\"ChronologyTeotihuacan2\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('ChronologyTeotihuacan2', '0%')\"><div class=\"htSlideCaption\">View of the Sun Pyramid in Teotihuacan, Mexico, from the Moon Pyramid. Photograph taken on <htdate2> during a site visit (<htcite19>).</div><div class=\"htSlideRefs\"><b>Related content:</b><ul><li>Understanding the Multiplication Tables (Summary) - After studying 10 multiplication tables, we will review the content. But now, we will follow the opposite direction, starting with charts until we reach the general formula. (<htcite20>)</li><li>Translation game (Part I) - Explore numbers from different cultures through an engaging translation game. (<htcite21>)</li></ul></div></div><i class=\"fa-solid fa-chevron-left htSlidePrev\" onclick=\"htPlusDivs(-1);\"></i><i class=\"fa-solid fa-chevron-right htSlideNext\" onclick=\"htPlusDivs(1);\"></i></div></p>",
